UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'TEMPLER 1977 DOES NOT RESOLVE TO ANY INDEXED ITEM, AND THE SURNAME IS NOW SETTLED AS John A. Templer, A STAIRS RESEARCHER. THE NEGATIVE IS WELL-FORMED, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT USABLE (R14). ERIC author:\"Templer\" returns 33 records -- a healthy result set, so the query shape is sound -- and NOT ONE is architectural: they are Bill Templer (education/politics), Lois Templer, Andrew J. Templer (management), Donald I. Templer (psychology, death-anxiety scales), Sally Templer. ERIC \"Templer\" AND \"stairs\" returns 0. A well-formed author query returning 33 and a topic conjunction returning 0 is GENUINE ABSENCE IN ERIC, not a query-shape failure -- and ERIC being an education index, absence there says nothing about the built-environment literature. P3b CORRECT AT 0.2. OpenAlex raw_author_name Templer + year 1977 returns 10, all of them Jerry W. Templer (ENT surgery), Donald I. Templer (psychology), Andrew J. Templer (management) or J.C. Templer. NO J.A. TEMPLER IN 1977 IN EITHER INDEX. P3a FALSIFIED: TRID served a 200 with a results page, and the page does not carry a single /View/ record id -- the markup is JS-rendered, so what came back is a shell. ''Templer'' appears 5 times in the body but no record is extractable. NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT TRID HOLDS; treat this as a blocked route like Jisc, not as a null. WHAT DID RESOLVE, VIA CROSSREF ON THE DISSERTATION TITLE REF-01005''S OWN BIBLIOGRAPHY CARRIES: John A. Templer''s indexed corpus is stairs, end to end -- ''An analysis of the behavior of stair users'', John A Templer, Gary M Mullet, John Archea, Stephen T Margulis, 1978, NBS internal report, DOI 10.6028/nbs.ir.78-1554; ''The Forgiving Stair'', 1984, Human Factors Society; ''The Soft Stair: Falls Induced by a Laboratory Stair'', 1989. Together with the 1974 dissertation ''Stair shape and human movement'' that REF-01005''s bibliography actually lists, that is FOUR Templer items spanning fifteen years and NOT ONE OF THEM IS ABOUT RAMPS, and not one is dated 1977. P3e ANSWERED IN AN UNEXPECTED DIRECTION: the ambiguity is not between several 1977 Templers, it is that there is no 1977 Templer at all and the nearest neighbours are 1974 and 1978. STILL NOT GUESSED, PER THE ASSIGNMENT: the 1978 NBS report is one year off and would be a plausible preprint-dated citation, and the 1974 dissertation is the one the report itself lists. Either could be footnote c''s referent and this pass does not choose. THE STRUCTURAL FINDING IS INDEPENDENT OF WHICH ONE IT IS: Table 13''s footnote c warrants the 1:8 and 1:10 rampway bands on two named parties, and one of them has no ramp research to his name. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: RETRACTED: THE UNIVERSAL NEGATIVE IN THIS NOTE IS FALSE. This pass concluded that "John A. Templer corpus is stairs, end to end", "FOUR ITEMS ACROSS FIFTEEN YEARS AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS ABOUT RAMPS", and that "one of them has no ramp research to his name". Found false by an adversarial pass over this same batch, and the route that finds it is one this session used the same hour for a different target. TEMPLER HAS SUBSTANTIAL RAMP RESEARCH: Templer, John A., Provisions for elderly and handicapped pedestrians, FHWA-RD-79-1 (Jan 1979), -2 and -3 (May 1980), Georgia Institute of Technology Pedestrian Research Laboratory, contract DOT-FH-11-8504, OCLC 5844243, full text on Internet Archive. Volume 3 contains a Part I titled SHORT RAMPS, TACTILE SURFACES AND WHEELCHAIR DIMENSIONS opening with "A Study of Short Ramps"; 32 gradient-rating tables (manual wheelchair users, electric wheelchair users, cane users, ambulant disabled, able-bodied, ascent and descent); and TABLE 20, "Ramp Gradient Recommendations", recommending against curb heights of 3, 6 and 9 inches across gradients 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16. Derive it: curl the archive.org advancedsearch endpoint for creator:Templer AND (pedestrian OR ramp OR handicapped) -- 3 results -- then count ramp in provisionsforeld00temp_0_djvu.txt, which returns 998. HOW THE ERROR WAS MADE, because the shape matters more than the fact. (1) THE QUERY THAT "RESOLVED HIS CORPUS" CONTAINED THE WORD STAIR. It was query.bibliographic=Templer+stair+shape+human+movement. A query containing stair returning stair papers is the query shape, not the corpus -- which is R14, the rule this same note invokes against ERIC two sentences earlier, applied to everyone except myself. (2) archive.org was never pointed at Templer, though this batch used exactly that endpoint for Walter at 03:10. (3) ERIC returned numFound 33 and the request carried rows=25, so 25 docs were examined and EIGHT WERE NEVER SEEN; "33 records, none architectural" overstates what was looked at, and results_screened=43 is a hand-typed figure the artefact contradicts. WHAT THIS DOES TO THE BATCH CONCLUSIONS: candidate 115 correction stands as far as it goes (the bibliography Templer is the 1974 Columbia stairs dissertation, and footnote c cites 1977) but its reasoning is void; candidate 117 stages the wrong neighbour; and the inference that "the priority of retrieving Templer drops" is INVERTED -- Table 20 recommends over 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16, the SAME FOUR BANDS as Steinfeld Table 13, and footnote c marks the two Steinfeld did not test. That is now the strongest available lead on what footnote c means. Candidate 125 stages it. STILL NOT GUESSED: Volume 3 is dated May 1980 and footnote c says 1977; the project field tests ran to Sep 1977, so an interim report of the same contract is plausible and unverified. || ADMISSION LINKED 2026-09-25 by session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa: REF-01008 (candidate 125): Candidate 125 was surfaced by this search and admitted as REF-01008 in batch 20 by retrieving its locator. No verb could attach an admission to an already-logged search, so the edge was missing and research_protocol_audit reported REF-01008 as admitted by no search. Written with link-admission (GAP-046), which refuses unless the candidate row records both ends, as S01 requires. || CORRECTED 2026-09-26: BATCH 20''s ADMISSION LINE ABOVE OVERSTATES THE PROVENANCE. It says ''Candidate 125 was surfaced by this search''. This search''s logged query names seven routes (ERIC, OpenAlex, Crossref, TRID) and not Internet Archive, where batch 19''s adversarial pass found the FHWA report (GAP-037''s 2026-09-20 text; the correction above on this note records the find). The edge (90, REF-01008) copies the candidate''s filing on this exec and is not independent evidence of it; results_admitted here is deliberately NOT raised to meet it. See GAP-050.' WHERE "exec_id" = 90;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'P6a CONFIRMED AND IT WAS THE LOW-PROBABILITY BRANCH. Admitted REF-01006 -- Raghuram, Verma, Lavalekar, Virk, Bhan and Singh 2026, ''Participatory accessibility audits as a tool for disability-inclusive health systems: Findings from two Indian states'', Dialogues in Health, doi 10.1016/j.dialog.2026.100342, PMC13543994, open access, full text retrieved as JATS XML and persisted. CO-1 AT THREE LEVELS, EVIDENCED IN THE RETRIEVED BYTES PER D-0178 RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM VENUE: research team composed primarily of persons with disabilities; a Community Advisory Board of disability rights advocates; and ALL field investigators persons with disabilities, audits run with a DPO. Named as Community Based Participatory Research, closing on ''Nothing about us, Without us''. IT STATES A GRADIENT AND THE STATEMENT MUST NOT BE OVER-READ: 1:12 is a yardstick taken from the Harmonised Guidelines and Standards for Universal Accessibility in India (2021), not a figure this study derived, so it is NOT Co-1 evidence that 1:12 is right. What it measures is compliance: 20 of 35 government hospitals (57.1 percent) at 1:12 or gentler, 17 of 24 in Madhya Pradesh, 3 of 11 in Goa. Recorded as an R7 failure finding. P6b UNTESTED BY THIS PASS RATHER THAN CONFIRMED, and the distinction matters: every query here was date-unbounded or post-2000, and none interrogated the pre-1990 layer, so nothing was learned about whether disabled-authored ramp work exists in that period. It remains an open question and this pass should not be cited as having settled it. P6c NOT THE RESULT, and I would have accepted it too readily: I pre-committed to reporting a silence as a finding, and a source turned up instead. Recording that the low-probability branch fired is the honest outcome, and it is a caution against the shape of P6c -- a prior that makes a null valuable is a prior that makes looking harder feel unnecessary. THE JURISDICTION IS NEW: India, on a slug whose Co-1 rows were both Japanese. ERIC returned 0 for ''wheelchair users'' AND ''ramp'', consistent with batch 18 finding ERIC topically thin on built-environment work while holding ED184280 by deposit. || ADMISSION LINKED 2026-09-25 by session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa: REF-01007 (candidate 124): Candidate 124 was surfaced by this search and admitted as REF-01007 in batch 20 by retrieving its locator. No verb could attach an admission to an already-logged search, so the edge was missing and research_protocol_audit reported REF-01007 as admitted by no search. Written with link-admission (GAP-046), which refuses unless the candidate row records both ends, as S01 requires. || CORRECTED 2026-09-26: BATCH 20''s ADMISSION LINE ABOVE OVERSTATES THE PROVENANCE. It says ''Candidate 124 was surfaced by this search''. Batch 19''s own record (sessions/session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer.md, section 13) says Pinto 2021 -- and Garg 2024, candidate 123 -- were found in REF-01006''s REFERENCE LIST, the source this search admitted, not in this search''s results; that backward step was never logged as a search. Candidates 123 and 124 were filed on this exec as the nearest logged search. The edge (91, REF-01007) copies that filing and is not independent evidence of it; results_admitted here is deliberately NOT raised to meet it. See GAP-050.' WHERE "exec_id" = 91;